<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="{{ page.site.description }}">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#244c3b">
  <meta property="og:type" content="article">
  <meta property="og:title" content="{{ manifest.title }}">
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{ page.site.description }}">
  <meta property="og:url" content="{{ page.site.canonical_url }}">
  <meta name="citation_title" content="{{ manifest.title }}">
  {% for author in manifest.authors %}<meta name="citation_author" content="{{ author }}">{% endfor %}
  <meta name="citation_journal_title" content="Transactions on Machine Learning Research">
  <meta name="citation_publication_date" content="2026/10">
  <meta name="citation_pdf_url" content="{{ page.site.canonical_url }}assets/pdfs/minerva-camera-ready.pdf">
  <title>Minerva · Reinforcement Learning for CTI · TMLR 2026</title>
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ page.site.canonical_url }}">
  <link rel="icon" href="static/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset_urls.get('static/index.css', 'static/index.css') }}">
  <script src="{{ asset_urls.get('static/index.js', 'static/index.js') }}" defer></script>
</head>
<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
  <header class="site-nav">
    <div class="nav-inner">
      <a class="wordmark" href="#top" aria-label="Minerva, back to top"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 21V3l9 10 9-10v18M3 3l9 18L21 3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/></svg>Minerva<span class="nav-venue">TMLR ’26</span></a>
      <nav aria-label="Page sections"><a href="#method">Method</a><a href="#results">Results</a><a href="#citation">Cite</a><a class="nav-code" href="{{ page.links.code }}">Code <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></nav>
    </div>
  </header>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" id="top" aria-labelledby="paper-title">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="venue"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ page.site.venue_line }}</p>
        <h1 id="paper-title"><span class="title-name">Minerva:</span> Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards<br class="desktop-break"> for Cyber Threat Intelligence LLMs</h1>
        <div class="authors">
          {% for author in manifest.author_entries %}<span class="author">{{ author.name }}<sup>{{ author.affiliation_indices|join(',') }}</sup></span>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        <div class="affiliations">
          {% for affiliation in page.site.affiliation_labels %}<span><sup>{{ loop.index }}</sup>{{ affiliation }}</span>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        <div class="hero-links">
          {% for item in page.site.cta_links %}<a class="button {% if loop.first %}button-primary{% endif %}" href="{{ asset_urls.get(page.links[item.key], page.links[item.key]) }}">{% if loop.first %}<svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 2h7l4 4v12H5V2zm7 0v5h4M8 11h5M8 14h5"/></svg>{% endif %}{{ item.label }}<span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        <figure class="hero-figure">
          <a href="{{ hero_asset }}" class="image-link" aria-label="Open the Minerva overview illustration at full size"><img src="{{ asset_urls.get(hero_asset, hero_asset) }}" width="1200" height="510" alt="{{ page.hero.alt }}" fetchpriority="high"></a>
          <figcaption><span>{{ page.hero.caption }}</span><a href="{{ hero_asset }}">Open overview <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="intro container" aria-labelledby="idea-title">
      <div class="intro-label"><p class="eyebrow">The idea</p><h2 id="idea-title">Better answers start<br>with better feedback.</h2></div>
      <div class="intro-copy"><p>CTI analysts turn messy security information into structured outputs: weakness identifiers, attack techniques, severity vectors, and more. These outputs can often be checked against public standards, making CTI a natural setting for <strong>reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards</strong>.</p><p>Minerva pairs a curated training suite with <strong>MinervaRL</strong>, a method that supplements GRPO with verified teacher traces when a model struggles to produce a fully correct answer.</p><details class="abstract"><summary>Read the full abstract <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>{{ manifest.abstract }}</p></details></div>
    </section>
    <div class="container">
      <dl class="highlights">{% for item in page.highlights['items'] %}<div><dt>{{ item.label }}</dt><dd>{{ item.value }}{% if item.value.startswith('+') %}<span>pp</span>{% endif %}</dd></div>{% endfor %}</dl>
      <p class="metric-note">Score improvements are averaged across four backbones and 12 evaluation benchmarks. pp = percentage points.</p>
    </div>

    {% for block in story_blocks %}
    {% set section = page.story.sections[loop.index0] %}
    <section class="story-section {% if section.kind in ['method','dynamics'] %}section-tinted{% endif %}" id="{{ section.id }}" aria-labelledby="{{ section.id }}-title">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">{{ section.eyebrow }}</p><h2 id="{{ section.id }}-title">{{ block.title }}</h2><p class="section-lead">{{ block.body }}</p></div>

        {% if section.kind == 'dataset' %}
        <div class="task-grid">
          <article class="task"><div class="task-symbol symbol-blue" aria-hidden="true">{ }</div><h3>Vulnerability mapping</h3><p>Map a CVE description to a canonical weakness or a CVSS severity vector.</p><div class="task-example"><span>CVE description</span><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><strong>CWE / CVSS</strong></div></article>
          <article class="task"><div class="task-symbol symbol-amber" aria-hidden="true">&lt;/&gt;</div><h3>Detection engineering</h3><p>Connect detection rules and emulation tests to ATT&amp;CK techniques.</p><div class="task-example"><span>Rule or test</span><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><strong>ATT&amp;CK technique</strong></div></article>
          <article class="task"><div class="task-symbol symbol-green" aria-hidden="true">↗</div><h3>Adversary behavior</h3><p>Identify techniques, tactics, and mitigations from attack scenarios.</p><div class="task-example"><span>Attack scenario</span><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><strong>Standardized identifiers</strong></div></article>
          <article class="task"><div class="task-symbol symbol-purple" aria-hidden="true">⌘</div><h3>Threat context</h3><p>Link actor behavior and CAPEC examples to structured security knowledge.</p><div class="task-example"><span>Behavior or example</span><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><strong>Actor / pattern / weakness</strong></div></article>
        </div>
        <p class="source-line">Representative task groups. Sources include MITRE ATT&amp;CK/CWE/CAPEC, CVE/NVD, Mappings Explorer, Sigma, Atomic Red Team, Microsoft Sentinel, and Splunk Security Content.</p>

        {% elif section.kind == 'method' %}
        <ol class="method-steps">
          <li><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Sample and verify</h3><p>Generate eight on-policy rollouts per prompt and score them with the task verifier. Continue the standard GRPO update.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Help the hard prompts</h3><p>If no rollout is fully verified, an EMA teacher generates traces conditioned on the target labels. Verify them and filter for quality.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Learn on the original prompt</h3><p>Periodically distill accepted traces onto the original prompt, without the target labels. This adds a supervised learning signal.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="method-note"><span class="note-mark" aria-hidden="true">↻</span><p><strong>The distinction matters.</strong> Labels help generate the teacher trace; the student learns from the original, answer-free prompt. The auxiliary update supplements the GRPO objective.</p></div>
        <details class="figure-details"><summary>Explore the full method diagram from the paper <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><figure><a href="{{ block.asset_images[0] }}"><img src="{{ asset_urls.get(block.asset_images[0], block.asset_images[0]) }}" alt="The standard GRPO update and the auxiliary ACR generation, filtering, buffer, and periodic distillation paths." loading="lazy" width="900" height="650"></a><figcaption>Solid arrows show synchronous training; dashed arrows show periodic distillation. Click the figure to enlarge.</figcaption></figure></details>

        {% elif section.kind == 'results' %}
        <figure class="results-figure"><a href="{{ block.asset_images[0] }}" class="image-link" aria-label="Open the benchmark comparison chart"><img src="{{ asset_urls.get(block.asset_images[0], block.asset_images[0]) }}" alt="Llama-3.1-8B: Base 48.6, GRPO 56.0, MinervaRL 60.2. Llama-3.2-3B: 33.1, 42.1, 48.3. Qwen3-8B: 37.6, 47.0, 53.4. Qwen3-4B: 27.6, 47.6, 48.0." loading="lazy" width="1120" height="570"></a></figure>
        <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Average benchmark scores, scroll horizontally on small screens" tabindex="0"><table class="summary-table"><caption>All controlled baselines · 12-task macro average</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Backbone</th>{% for method in page.results.methods %}<th scope="col" {% if method == 'MinervaRL' %}class="best-column"{% endif %}>{{ method }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead><tbody>{% for row in page.results.summary_rows %}<tr><th scope="row">{{ row.backbone }}</th>{% for value in row.scores %}<td {% if loop.last %}class="best-column"{% endif %}>{% if loop.last %}<strong>{{ '%.1f'|format(value) }}</strong>{% else %}{{ '%.1f'|format(value) }}{% endif %}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
        <p class="source-line">Paper Table 1. Scores are percentages; the macro average combines the 12 task metrics. Llama backbones start from Instruct checkpoints; Qwen3 backbones start from Base checkpoints. STaR, DART, and LUFFY are the CTI adaptations used in the paper.</p>
        <details class="full-results"><summary>View scores for all 12 benchmarks <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Per-task scores, scroll horizontally" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Full scores for the six controlled variants of each backbone</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Backbone / method</th>{% for task in page.results.tasks %}<th scope="col">{{ task }}</th>{% endfor %}<th scope="col">Avg.</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for row in page.results.rows %}<tr {% if row.method == 'MinervaRL' %}class="minerva-row"{% endif %}><th scope="row">{{ row.backbone }} / {{ row.method }}</th>{% for task in page.results.tasks %}<td>{{ '%.1f'|format(row.scores[task]) }}</td>{% endfor %}<td>{{ '%.1f'|format(row.average) }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div><p class="source-line">The paper additionally reports Llama-Primus-Merged and Foundation-Sec-8B-Instruct as external security-SFT references. Consult Table 1 for those two rows.</p><a class="text-link" href="assets/data/results.json" download>Download the reported data (JSON) ↓</a></details>
        <div class="uncertainty"><div><p class="eyebrow">Beyond the average</p><h3>34 of 48 point comparisons<br>favor MinervaRL over GRPO.</h3><p>Task-level gains vary. Qwen3-4B has the smallest mean gain, and some tasks regress. Paired 95% bootstrap intervals give a more careful view:</p></div><div class="ci-chart"><div class="ci-bar" role="img" aria-label="28 of 48 comparisons have a positive interval, 13 overlap zero, and 7 have a negative interval"><span class="ci-positive" style="flex:28">28</span><span class="ci-overlap" style="flex:13">13</span><span class="ci-negative" style="flex:7">7</span></div><div class="ci-legend"><span><i class="ci-positive"></i>Above zero</span><span><i class="ci-overlap"></i>Overlaps zero</span><span><i class="ci-negative"></i>Below zero</span></div><p class="source-line">Intervals reflect variation over evaluation examples for fixed checkpoints, rather than variation across training seeds.</p></div></div>

        {% elif section.kind == 'dynamics' %}
        <div class="plot-stack">
          {% for image in block.asset_images %}<figure><div class="plot-label"><span class="plot-index">{{ '%02d'|format(loop.index) }}</span><h3>{% if loop.first %}Prompts with no verifier reward{% else %}Validation accuracy during training{% endif %}</h3></div><a href="{{ image }}" class="image-link" aria-label="Open {% if loop.first %}reward sparsity{% else %}validation accuracy{% endif %} plot at full size"><img src="{{ asset_urls.get(image, image) }}" alt="{% if loop.first %}Paper training curves comparing GRPO and MinervaRL across the four backbones. Llama-3.1-8B also includes the GRPO-12 rollout control.{% else %}Paper validation curves for four backbones. Dashed lines mark best GRPO accuracy and crosses mark the first MinervaRL step to reach it.{% endif %}" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>{% if loop.first %}Fraction of prompts whose rollout group has maximum reward zero. This diagnostic differs from the method’s no-fully-verified-rollout gate.{% else %}Curves use five-step rolling means. Dashed lines mark best GRPO accuracy; crosses mark when MinervaRL first reaches it.{% endif %}</figcaption></figure>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        <p class="compute-note"><strong>Progress has a compute cost.</strong> In the paper’s 10-step Llama-3.1-8B timing study, MinervaRL took 654.5 seconds versus 469.8 for GRPO, a 39.3% increase. Earlier progress in training steps is not a claim of lower cost per step.</p>

        {% elif section.kind == 'scope' %}
        <div class="scope-grid"><article><h3>What the study establishes</h3><p>Verifiable CTI tasks provide useful reward signals, and auxiliary self-training improves aggregate performance across the evaluated backbone groups.</p></article><article><h3>What remains open</h3><p>Independent training-seed variation, broader gate and hyperparameter ablations, multilingual CTI, and reducing the cost of trace generation and filtering.</p></article></div>
        <p class="defensive-note">Minerva is intended for defensive CTI analysis. Its outputs support analyst judgment and require validation before operational use.</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </section>
    {% endfor %}

    <section class="citation-section" id="citation" aria-labelledby="citation-title"><div class="container"><div class="citation-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Build on this work</p><h2 id="citation-title">Citation</h2><p>Accepted in <em>Transactions on Machine Learning Research</em>, 2026.</p></div><button class="button copy-button" type="button" id="copy-citation">Copy BibTeX <span aria-hidden="true">⧉</span></button></div><pre><code id="bibtex">{{ generated_bibtex }}</code></pre><p class="copy-status" id="copy-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><div class="closing-links"><a href="{{ page.links.paper_pdf }}">Read the full paper ↗</a><a href="{{ page.links.code }}">Explore the code ↗</a><a href="{{ page.links.openreview }}">OpenReview discussion ↗</a></div></div></section>
  </main>
  <footer><div class="container footer-inner"><p><span class="footer-wordmark">Minerva</span> · Verifiable learning for cyber threat intelligence.</p><p><a href="{{ page.links.page_source }}">Page source</a> · Built with <a href="https://github.com/maveryn/paperpage">paperpage</a></p></div></footer>
</body>
</html>
